## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Observed 2026-08-30 by the B056 lane while pinning the module-name scope rule.
Recorded as an observation, not acted on: no corpus row depends on it, and it
was found mid-gate on an unrelated ticket.

## Observation

`CMwNod` behaves as a live AngelScript scope in the game, but
`GlobalScope::scope_exists` answers false for it. Same shape as the
`GameData` exception B056 documented, except in CLASS position rather than
module position.

## Why it may matter

B056 established, and the coordinator verified independently against all 20
Nadeo module names, that:

    a module name is a live scope  <=>  Core declares it as a namespace

with `GameData` the single unexplained exception (4 scopes: Audio, Meta, Net,
GameData; Audio/Meta/Net are Core namespaces, GameData is not). `CMwNod` is a
SECOND unexplained live scope, in a different position, which weakens the
'lone exception' reading — two exceptions in two positions is more likely a
missing rule than two quirks.

If the rule is actually something like 'a name is a scope when the engine
registers members under it', then both `GameData` and `CMwNod` may be
explained by the same mechanism, and `scope_exists` is under-approximating
rather than these being anomalies. Worth finding out before either is treated
as permanent.

## Work

Probe first, implement second. Establish what IS a scope in the live game
beyond the 20 module names — at minimum a paired `ZZZNoSuchEnumName` probe
across a sample of engine CLASS names, so the class-position rule is measured
rather than assumed from one observation.

Then decide whether `scope_exists` should recognise them. Note the
consequence direction: `scope_exists` answering false where the game says true
is a FALSE POSITIVE risk (the LSP rejects a spelling the game accepts), which
is the more damaging direction and the one B052/B056/B057 exist to remove.

Do NOT hardcode names. The B056 lane declined to match `GameData` for exactly
that reason — hardcoding an engine fact neither database records is how this
repo's earlier wrong numbers were made.

## Test plan

Whatever lands must pin the measured set the way
`a_module_name_is_a_scope_only_when_core_declares_it` does: assert the split
AND the exact count of exceptions, so a new exception goes red rather than
quietly widening what qualifies.

## Related

B056 (established the module-name rule and its one exception), B052
(ancestor-walk / scope gating), `docs/probes/b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv`
(the paired-probe corpus; extend it rather than starting a new one).

## MEASURED (coordinator, 2026-08-31) — this ticket's premise is WRONG

Ran the class-position campaign the Work section asked for, against the live
game (Openplanet 1.29.5 next/Public/1234ad9e). Corpus:
`docs/probes/b067-class-position-scopes.tsv`.

**`CMwNod` is not an exception. Every engine class is a live scope.**

41 engine classes sampled deterministically (seed 67), spread across all 20
modules, deliberately split between types that HAVE nested enums (`e` key, 134
in the dump) and types that do NOT (2451). **All 41 answered SCOPE** — the
`Identifier 'ZZZNoSuchName' is not a data type in namespace 'X' or parent`
discriminator, not `Namespace 'X' doesn't exist.` Having nested enums makes no
difference, so the hypothesis "a class is a scope when members are registered
under it" is refuted: classes with nothing under them are scopes too.

### The rule

    X is a live scope for `X::Y`  <=>  X is a declared namespace OR X is a known data type

Both halves measured, in isolated compilation units (see B068 for why that
matters):

| probe | verdict | evidence |
|---|---|---|
| `ZZZNoSuchClass::` | NOT a scope | `Namespace 'ZZZNoSuchClass' doesn't exist.` |
| `MwFoundations::` `Game::` `ShootMania::` | NOT scopes | same message — confirms B056's module rule |
| `Audio::` `Net::` `Meta::` `GameData::` | scopes | the 4 known live module scopes |
| `CMwNod::` `CGameAction::` `CAudioListener::` | scopes | re-verified individually |
| `string::` | scope | a VALUE type, not an engine class — so the rule is about types generally |
| `CMwNod` / `string` in type position | ARE data types | accepted as a declaration |

This is ordinary AngelScript: `Type::Nested` is scope-resolution syntax, so any
known type name opens a scope regardless of whether anything is in it.

### B056's exception SURVIVES, and is not paired with this

I tested the tempting unification — "GameData is a scope because it is secretly
a type" — and it is **false**:

    Identifier 'GameData' is not a data type in global namespace

Same for `Audio`, `Net`, `Meta`. So `GameData` is a scope while being neither a
Core-declared namespace (B056) nor a data type. It remains the single
unexplained module-position exception. The ticket's reasoning that "two
exceptions in two positions is more likely a missing rule" was right that a
rule was missing — but the missing rule explains `CMwNod` and every other type,
not `GameData`.

### Consequence for the fix

`scope_exists` under-approximates, and in the FALSE POSITIVE direction the
ticket correctly flags as the damaging one. It should answer true when the name
is a **known type in the loaded index**, not only when it is a namespace. That
is derivable from the index itself, so it satisfies "do NOT hardcode names" —
there is nothing to hardcode.

Scope note: this is `src/typedb/index.rs:707` and
`src/typecheck/global_scope.rs:481`. `index.rs` is lane-b057's file for the
B056 follow-up, so **this ticket stays blocked until that lane lands**, and
whoever takes it should read lane-b057's resolution of where the
`scope_exists(ns)` gate sits before touching it.

### Honest limits

- 41 of 2585 types is a sample, not a census. All 41 agreed and the mechanism
  explains why it should be universal, but it is a sample. A census is cheap if
  ever wanted.
- The 41 came from one batch. That batch is trustworthy **because all 41
  produced distinct diagnostics** — a parse abort (B068) yields zero, not some.
  Three were re-verified in isolation regardless.
- Measured on one install, one Openplanet build. A probe bounds behaviour, not
  intent.
- The follow-up run to re-verify the B052 corpus could not complete: the game
  exited mid-run. See B068.